  THE COST, paired on seed against `b0`
    top7  -0.0889 +/- 0.0080  (-11.1 SE)   53% of the lesson
    bot2  -0.0144 +/- 0.0099  (-1.5 SE)   9% of the lesson
    bot4  -0.0171 +/- 0.0095  (-1.8 SE)   10% of the lesson
    bot7  -0.0153 +/- 0.0102  (-1.5 SE)   9% of the lesson

  THE TWO HALVES, paired on seed (positive = the LOWER half is cheaper to
  block, i.e. the lesson does better keeping the UPPER half)
    bot7 - top7  +0.0737 +/- 0.0086  (+8.6 SE)

  THE LEVERAGE IS CONCENTRATED AT ONE END, AND ONLY ONE LESSON CAN HAVE
  IT (8.6 SE). Blocking the lower half costs 9% of the lesson while
  blocking the upper half costs 53%. Both are the same SEVEN neurons;
  only which end differs.

  READ WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT SAY. It does NOT make separation
  affordable: separation needs both halves working at once, one lesson in
  each, and the half without the leverage is crippled. But it is a much
  sharper diagnosis than "confining a lesson is unaffordable" -- the
  problem is that `read_group` puts all the steering authority at one end
  of the group, so the two halves are not interchangeable.

  AND IT NAMES A DIFFERENT FIX. Do not look for a cheaper place to put a
  mask; make the readout's leverage UNIFORM. A position map that is not
  monotone in index, or a readout that is not a weighted mean over a
  contiguous slice, would give both halves equal authority -- and that is
  a change to the decoder rather than to the teaching.
